You are the lead product designer and senior frontend engineer responsible for redesigning the HIVE command center.

Do not merely produce a design concept, mockup, theme file, or list of recommendations. Inspect the existing repository, run the application, identify the current visual problems, redesign the relevant components, implement the new interface, verify the live data flows, test the 3D overlays, and leave the application running with a polished production-quality interface.

The result should feel as though an Apple product team designed an industrial operations system.

This does not mean copying Apple websites, adding excessive glass, or turning every button into a capsule. It means applying Apple’s actual strengths:

* Ruthless visual hierarchy
* Excellent typography
* Precise spacing
* Quiet surfaces
* Semantic color
* Meaningful depth
* Physically coherent motion
* High information density without visual clutter
* Controls that feel native and intentional
* 3D content that communicates state rather than decorating the screen

HIVE is an AI operating system for physical organizations. The host interface receives a high-level objective, observes a physical workspace through a webcam, builds a live world model, coordinates five human workers, verifies actions, detects deviations, and replans in real time.

The interface must make this intelligence understandable without resorting to generic AI visual clichés.

# 1. First inspect the existing product

Before changing code:

1. Inspect the frontend architecture, routes, components, state management, WebSocket client, camera implementation, simulation view, task graph, and styling.
2. Run the full frontend and backend.
3. Open `/host`, `/join`, and `/worker/:id`.
4. Capture screenshots at:

   * 1440 × 900
   * 1728 × 1117
   * 1024 × 768
   * 390 × 844
5. Identify every instance of:

   * Glowing dots
   * Neon borders
   * Decorative gradients
   * Oversized rounded cards
   * Capsule buttons
   * Rounded-square icon containers
   * Fake glassmorphism
   * Random blur
   * Excessive shadows
   * “AI” particle effects
   * Dotted grid backgrounds
   * Floating widgets with no hierarchy
   * Monospace text used as decoration
   * Tiny illegible labels
   * Unnecessary all-caps text
   * Repeated status pills
   * Generic dashboard card grids
   * Motion that loops without conveying state
6. Remove or replace these patterns systematically rather than layering another theme on top of them.

Do not preserve weak components merely because they already exist.

# 2. Core visual direction

The interface should feel like a combination of:

* A native macOS professional application
* A high-end industrial control surface
* An Apple spatial-computing interface
* A live digital twin of the physical workspace

It must not resemble:

* A crypto dashboard
* A gaming HUD
* A cyberpunk interface
* A Dribbble analytics dashboard
* A generic Tailwind admin template
* A “vibe-coded AI” landing page
* A military radar screen
* A neon mission-control concept
* A collection of floating cards

Use a near-black neutral environment, but do not make the screen visually dead.

Recommended foundation:

```css
--background: #0b0b0c;
--surface-primary: #141416;
--surface-secondary: #1c1c1e;
--surface-elevated: #242426;

--text-primary: #f5f5f7;
--text-secondary: #a1a1a6;
--text-tertiary: #6e6e73;

--separator: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
--separator-strong: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.14);

--hive-accent: #f2c94c;
--success: #30d158;
--warning: #ff9f0a;
--failure: #ff453a;
--information: #0a84ff;
```

These values are starting points, not an excuse to scatter color throughout the interface.

Color rules:

* Default UI is neutral and mostly monochrome.
* Yellow is HIVE’s identity color and primary-action accent.
* Green appears only for verified success or healthy completion.
* Red appears only for active failures, destructive actions, or emergency states.
* Blue appears only for informational selection or observed data.
* Do not color each panel differently.
* Do not assign workers five loud colors across the full interface.
* Worker colors may appear as one small line, dot, or object identifier.
* Never use gradients.
* Never use outer glows.
* Never use colored shadows.
* Never use blurred color blobs.
* Never use animated ambient backgrounds.

# 3. Typography

Use the native Apple system stack:

```css
font-family:
  -apple-system,
  BlinkMacSystemFont,
  "SF Pro Display",
  "SF Pro Text",
  "Helvetica Neue",
  Arial,
  sans-serif;
```

Do not download or bundle Apple font files.

Typography should create hierarchy without relying on decorative containers.

Suggested scale:

```text
Primary workspace title: 28–32px, weight 600
Objective or critical state: 22–26px, weight 600
Section heading: 15–17px, weight 600
Body and control labels: 14–16px, weight 400–500
Metadata: 12–13px, weight 400–500
Technical timestamps: 11–12px, tabular numbers
```

Rules:

* Use sentence case.
* Avoid excessive uppercase.
* “HIVE” may remain uppercase as the product name.
* Emergency states may use concise uppercase labels.
* Use tabular numerals for timers, confidence values, and counts.
* Do not use monospace for normal labels.
* Monospace is permitted only for IDs, logs, structured payloads, and technical coordinates.
* Limit text weights. Most of the product should use regular or medium.
* Avoid huge “marketing website” typography inside the application.
* Make critical information readable from several feet away.

# 4. Overall application architecture

Do not build the host page as a grid of dashboard cards.

Create one coherent workspace with four structural regions:

```text
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Global toolbar                                               │
├───────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬───────────────┤
│ Operations    │                              │ Inspector     │
│ sidebar       │  Live spatial workspace      │               │
│               │  Camera + 3D world model     │               │
│               │                              │               │
├───────────────┴──────────────────────────────┴───────────────┤
│ Execution timeline / event stream                            │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

## Global toolbar

The toolbar should contain:

* HIVE wordmark
* Current system state
* Current objective title
* Worker connection count
* Camera or simulation mode
* Pause
* Start or continue
* One restrained overflow menu

The toolbar should be approximately 52–60px high.

Do not place every action in the toolbar.

Do not wrap every toolbar icon in a pill.

Use small rounded rectangles for grouped controls where necessary. Preserve horizontal density.

## Left operations sidebar

The left sidebar should contain:

* Current objective
* Plan status
* Five workers
* Current assignment per worker
* Availability
* A compact progress summary
* Goal input or scenario selection before execution

The workers should be presented as a calm vertical list, not five floating cards.

Each worker row should include:

* Worker name
* Small identity marker
* Current instruction or “Stand by”
* Concise state
* Optional elapsed time
* One contextual action menu

A worker row should become slightly elevated or highlighted only when selected.

## Central spatial workspace

This is the visual center of the product.

It contains:

* Live webcam feed or simulation view
* Actual working 3D overlay
* Object anchors
* Target zones
* Predicted movement paths
* Current object locks
* Verification state
* Selected task context
* Deviation visualization

This area should occupy most of the screen.

The camera feed should not be buried inside a small card.

The world model is the product.

## Right inspector

The inspector changes based on selection.

When nothing is selected, show:

* Current execution phase
* Next important dependency
* Verification confidence
* Planner source
* Current system explanation

When a worker is selected, show:

* Worker state
* Current action
* Reachable zones
* Assignment reason
* Controls relevant to that worker

When an object is selected, show:

* Object identity
* Observed position
* Expected position
* Confidence
* Current lock
* Related action

When an action is selected, show:

* Dependencies
* Assigned worker
* Expected predicates
* Evidence
* Recovery options

Do not display all inspector content simultaneously.

## Bottom timeline

Use a single, horizontally readable execution timeline.

Show:

* Time
* Event
* Actor
* Status
* Verification result

Allow expansion for technical detail.

Avoid chat bubbles.

Avoid a giant scrolling terminal.

# 5. Surfaces and containers

Use containers only where they create meaningful grouping.

Rules:

* Primary layout separators should be one-pixel lines.
* Avoid surrounding every section with a border.
* Major surfaces may use 14–18px corner radii.
* Controls may use 7–10px corner radii.
* Do not use fully rounded controls except:

  * Native switches
  * Small status indicators
  * Compact segmented selections where appropriate
* Avoid nested cards.
* Avoid borders plus shadows plus blur on the same element.
* Use one subtle elevation treatment at most.
* Use opacity, spacing, and separators before adding containers.
* Avoid frosted glass over a dark abstract background.
* If translucency is used, it must reveal meaningful underlying content, such as the webcam or 3D scene.

# 6. Icons and controls

Use a consistent icon library with visual proportions similar to SF Symbols.

Lucide may be used if already installed, but configure it consistently:

* 1.5px or equivalent stroke
* 16–18px default size
* 20px for prominent controls
* Rounded line caps
* No icon-specific colorful backgrounds
* No oversized icon tiles

Never place every icon inside a rounded square.

Use icon-only buttons only when the action is universally understandable and has an accessible label.

Primary actions:

* Use one yellow filled rounded rectangle.
* Use dark text on the yellow action.
* Do not make multiple actions visually primary.
* “Start Execution” may be primary.
* “Pause” should be neutral until active.
* “Emergency Stop” should be red, clearly separated, and require deliberate activation.

Secondary actions should be text, neutral icons, or quiet bordered controls.

# 7. Motion system

Motion must explain what changed.

Allowed motion:

* A selected object smoothly moves from its observed position to its updated position.
* A new assignment line draws from a worker to an action.
* A completed action settles into its final state.
* Inspector content crossfades when selection changes.
* A recovery path replaces the invalid path.
* A panel enters or exits with short opacity and position motion.
* Verification confidence resolves from pending to verified.
* The 3D camera subtly reframes selected content.

Forbidden motion:

* Constant pulsing dots
* Infinite breathing glows
* Random particle movement
* Animated grid backgrounds
* Decorative scanning lines
* Continuous floating cards
* Wobbling buttons
* Excessive spring bounce
* Repeated icon rotation
* Full-screen flashing

Timing:

```text
Hover feedback: 100–140ms
Selection changes: 160–220ms
Panel transitions: 180–260ms
Task reassignment: 280–420ms
Major replan visualization: 500–800ms
```

Use physically plausible easing.

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

Motion should never delay execution or hide important information.

# 8. Implement real 3D overlays

The 3D overlay cannot be a static illustration or a few CSS circles over the camera.

Use Three.js or the existing WebGL solution to create an actual scene rendered over the webcam.

## Required architecture

Create:

```text
Camera/video layer
        ↓
Transparent WebGL canvas
        ↓
3D world entities
        ↓
Screen-space labels and controls
```

The video element and renderer must:

* Share the same visible bounds
* Preserve the camera feed’s aspect ratio
* Remain synchronized through resizing
* Work in fullscreen and normal layouts
* Account for `object-fit` cropping
* Correctly map normalized detection coordinates to rendered coordinates

## Workspace calibration

Implement a simple four-point table calibration:

1. Host clicks the four visible corners of the physical workspace.
2. Store those points in normalized video coordinates.
3. Calculate a homography or equivalent plane transform.
4. Map world positions into a stable table coordinate system.
5. Save calibration for the current session.
6. Allow recalibration from Advanced Controls.

This should make target zones and movement paths align with the real table rather than floating arbitrarily over the image.

## Scene structure

Represent the table as a virtual plane.

Render:

* Object anchors
* Target zones
* Expected positions
* Movement paths
* Resource locks
* Verification outlines
* Selection outlines
* A subtle objective layout preview

Use simple geometry:

* Thin rings
* Flat planes
* Short vertical markers
* Clean path lines
* Minimal translucent fills
* Small labels
* Ghosted target geometry

Do not render glowing holograms.

Do not render large floating spheres.

Do not use random 3D particles.

Do not use complex environment maps merely for visual effect.

## Object anchors

Each tracked object should have:

* Stable identifier
* Current physical position
* Smoothed display position
* Confidence
* Selected state
* Expected destination
* Active action
* Lock state

Update observations at the vision system’s frequency, but render at the browser’s animation-frame frequency.

Use smoothing to prevent visual jitter:

```text
newDisplayPosition =
  previousDisplayPosition +
  smoothingFactor ×
  (observedPosition - previousDisplayPosition)
```

Do not smooth so aggressively that the overlay lags behind the physical object.

## Labels

Labels must remain legible and restrained.

Use small screen-space labels positioned relative to 3D anchors.

Labels may show:

* RED KIT
* Worker B
* Moving to Medical
* 91% observed

Do not show all metadata at once.

Show detailed information on selection or hover.

Avoid overlapping labels. Implement basic collision avoidance or priority rules.

## Paths

When HIVE dispatches an action:

* Draw a thin path from the object’s current location to its destination.
* Use neutral white or yellow for the active path.
* Fade inactive paths.
* Use a small directional marker.
* Animate the path once when it is created.
* Do not continuously pulse it.

When replanning:

* The invalid route fades out.
* The new route draws into place.
* The assigned worker indicator transitions to the new worker.
* The physical state remains visible throughout.

## Target zones

Target zones should feel attached to the table.

Use:

* Thin boundary
* Very low-opacity fill
* Small corner label
* Subtle depth offset

A zone becomes visually stronger only when:

* It is the current destination
* It contains a deviation
* It has been successfully verified
* The user selects it

## Verification

Pending verification:

* Neutral outline
* Small progress indicator
* “Awaiting verification”

Verified:

* Brief green resolution
* Then return to a quiet neutral state with a check symbol

Failed:

* Red outline
* Expected position remains visible
* Observed object is visibly separated
* A concise deviation message appears

Do not leave large green or red areas on screen after the state is resolved.

## Selection interaction

Support:

* Click object to inspect
* Click worker to see assignment
* Click action to highlight dependencies
* Click target zone to inspect expected predicates
* Drag simulated objects in simulation mode
* Raycasting against 3D objects
* Keyboard navigation for equivalent controls

## Performance

Requirements:

* 60 FPS rendering target on a normal laptop
* Vision updates must not recreate the scene
* Reuse geometries and materials
* Dispose removed resources
* Avoid allocating new vectors each frame
* Pause rendering when the page is hidden
* Do not call an AI model from the render loop
* Do not block WebSocket updates
* Degrade cleanly when WebGL is unavailable

Fallback:

* Render the same anchors and paths in a 2D Canvas or SVG layer
* Preserve all critical functionality
* Display a quiet “2D overlay” mode indicator

# 9. Simulation should match the camera view

Simulation mode must use the same world-model components as live mode.

Do not build a visually unrelated cartoon table.

The simulation should appear as a clean digital twin:

* Neutral table plane
* Simple physically shaded objects
* Identical zones
* Identical labels
* Identical task paths
* Identical verification states

Switching between camera and simulation should preserve:

* Object IDs
* Worker assignments
* Task graph
* Current actions
* Event timeline
* Inspector selection

The simulation is not a mockup. It is a first-class operational mode.

# 10. Task graph redesign

The task graph must be legible, but it should not dominate the entire interface when the spatial workspace is more important.

Present the graph as an expandable layer or mode.

Default graph view:

* Current action
* Immediate dependencies
* Next unlocked actions
* Blocked actions
* Recovery branch when applicable

Allow “Show full plan” to reveal the complete DAG.

Node design:

* Small rounded rectangles
* Clear title
* Worker assignment
* Status expressed through one edge or indicator
* No glowing nodes
* No colorful node backgrounds
* No animated particles moving through edges

Edges:

* Thin neutral lines
* Stronger for active dependencies
* Dashed only when the relationship is uncertain or proposed
* Red only for a broken dependency
* Green only during successful resolution

# 11. Replanning experience

This is the demo’s most important visual moment.

It must be dramatic through clarity, not visual noise.

When a deviation occurs:

1. The camera remains visible.
2. The incorrect object or unavailable worker is clearly identified.
3. Unaffected actions continue normally.
4. A restrained red status line appears:
   “Observed state differs from expected state.”
5. The affected dependency path is highlighted.
6. The old assignment is removed.
7. The new assignment is constructed visibly.
8. The updated movement path appears.
9. The status changes from:

   * Deviation detected
   * Evaluating constraints
   * Reassigning
   * Execution resumed

Do not cover the screen with a giant red modal.

Do not shake the UI.

Do not flash “REPLANNING” repeatedly.

Use a concise temporary state banner integrated into the toolbar or spatial workspace.

Example:

```text
Deviation detected
Yellow battery is outside the communications route.

Reassigning retrieval to Worker D
Reason: reachable, available, no active resource conflict
```

# 12. Objective and planning experience

Before execution, the workspace should feel calm and focused.

Show:

* One objective input
* Five connected workers
* Detected objects
* Camera readiness
* One “Compile Plan” action

After compilation:

* Replace the input-heavy state with a concise objective summary.
* Reveal only the most important plan information:

  * Actions
  * Parallel groups
  * Constraints
  * Estimated duration
* Let the user inspect the full graph when needed.

Do not preserve a large text input throughout execution.

# 13. Responsive behavior

Desktop is the presentation priority, but all routes must remain responsive.

## Host

At narrow desktop widths:

* Collapse right inspector into a drawer.
* Keep camera view dominant.
* Reduce sidebar width.
* Move timeline into a collapsible bottom sheet.

## Worker phone interface

The worker experience should be exceptionally simple.

Display:

* Worker identity
* Current instruction
* Current state
* One primary action
* Secondary help controls

Do not show the full dashboard.

Suggested structure:

```text
Worker B
Connected

Move the yellow battery
to Communications.

[Begin action]

Completed
Cannot complete
Repeat instruction
```

The instruction must be the visual focus.

Avoid pills and icon grids.

Use large readable text and large touch targets.

# 14. Advanced Controls

Advanced controls should not pollute the main interface.

Place them in a right-side inspector section or modal sheet.

Group by:

* Worker overrides
* Object overrides
* Action overrides
* Demo failure injection
* Camera calibration
* System reset

Use normal rows and controls.

Do not create a grid of colorful debug buttons.

Dangerous controls must be clearly separated.

# 15. Implementation standards

Use the existing React, TypeScript, Vite, WebSocket, and state architecture unless there is a concrete reason to refactor.

Requirements:

* Create reusable design tokens.
* Create consistent primitives for:

  * Toolbar button
  * Primary button
  * Secondary button
  * Inspector row
  * Worker row
  * Status indicator
  * Section heading
  * Timeline event
  * Empty state
  * Error state
* Do not introduce a heavyweight component library solely for appearance.
* Remove redundant CSS rather than layering overrides.
* Preserve keyboard navigation.
* Add visible focus states.
* Preserve contrast.
* Add accessible names to icon-only controls.
* Ensure live status changes use appropriate ARIA live regions.
* Keep WebSocket and orchestration behavior unchanged unless fixing a real bug.
* Do not replace real data with hard-coded demo data.
* Do not fake the 3D overlay with screenshots.
* Do not hide system failures.

# 16. Automated visual lint checks

Add a script or test that searches the frontend for common visual regressions.

Fail or warn on:

```text
linear-gradient
radial-gradient
drop-shadow with colored values
box-shadow values resembling glow
blurred colored background blobs
border-radius: 9999px outside approved controls
animate-pulse used for decoration
infinite animation outside loading indicators
random dotted background patterns
```

Allow exceptions only when documented.

